Z||000013||Master_Sushi||06-28-2001||11:09 AM||||""The LO Quad 4s (150-160 HP) came with either the T125 auto (like the Fieros) or the Getrag 5 speed.""<P>So would a Lo quad 4 bolt right up to a fiero auto trans? Even a low output would be a huge improvement from a duke. What was the RPM range on the low output quad 4? Did they take well to nitro/turbos? Im really not doing a swap in the near future, but Im sure I will one day. Just kind of interested.<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000014||pherder||06-28-2001||11:43 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by master_Sushi:<BR><B>So would a Lo quad 4 bolt right up to a Fiero auto trans? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Re-read what I wrote:<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>You MUST use the transaxle that comes with the engine because the bolt pattern for the bell housing is different from EVERY other GM vehicle.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You will be able to bolt the T125 from the LO Quad 4 into the Fiero AND use the Fiero's auto axles.<P>My First Quad 4 Fiero was exactly this combo.<P>It was a <B>DOG</B> until the rpms reached &gt; 3000.  No better than a regular 2.5 L4 Fiero <B>UNTIL</B> the rpms got up.<P>The LO Quad 4 w/5 speed WOULD be a nice compromise of great gas mileage and sporty engine AND you would only need the 87 octane fuel!  Yes, you <B>HAVE</B> to use Premium fuel with the HO Quad 4 or the ECM will retard the timing so much it is like driving a 2.5 L4 again!!!<P>Paul<P><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
